## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests advanced dynamic programming techniques involving multiple non-overlapping segments with per-element costs—a pattern frequently appearing in interviews for algorithm-intensive roles.

## Key Concepts
- Partition DP with fixed number of blocks requiring optimal segmentation
- Prefix sum transformations to simplify recurrence relations
- Time-space optimization strategies and solution reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValue(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const P = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) P[i+1] = P[i] + A[i];

  const B = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let x = 0; x <= n; x++) B[x] = P[x] - c * x;

  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;

  for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i][t-1];
      for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
        const candidate = dp[j][t-1] + B[i] - B[j];
        if (candidate > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = candidate;
          parent[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct solution
  const blocks = [];
  let curr = n, currT = k;
  while (currT > 0 && parent[curr][currT] !== -1) {
    const start = parent[curr][currT];
    blocks.unshift([start, curr-1]);
    curr = start;
    currT--;
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Implementation Details
- The transformation B[x] = P[x] - c*x captures the net value after maintenance costs
- The nested loop structure ensures we consider all possible segment boundaries
- Parent array enables O(k) reconstruction of optimal segment endpoints

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests a beginner-friendly DP pattern for selecting exactly k non-overlapping contiguous subarrays with a per-subarray cost. It forces candidates to choose between extending a current subarray and starting a new one, while also testing reconstruction and space-time trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- **DP state separation**: Maintaining 'best' (completed subarrays) vs 'active' (subarray currently being built)
- **Non-overlapping interval selection** with a fixed count constraint
- **Reconstruction** of the chosen subarrays from DP tables
- **Space optimization** using rolling arrays (2D → 1D)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const best = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const active = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));

  best[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      best[i][t] = Math.max(best[i-1][t], active[i-1][t]);

      if (t > 0) {
        active[i][t] = Math.max(
          active[i-1][t] + A[i-1],
          best[i-1][t-1] + A[i-1] - c
        );
      }
    }
  }

  return best[n][k];
}
```

**Time Complexity**: O(n × k)
**Space Complexity**: O(n × k), or O(k) with rolling arrays.

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes DP with non-overlapping blocks and a non-additive subarray score (bitwise OR). It requires a state that handles gaps and a compact representation of OR values, plus a reconstruction strategy.

## Key Concepts
- DP over k blocks with a required gap
- Distinct ORs per end i optimization (≤ O(log MAXA))
- Backtracking pointers for block endpoints
- Time/space trade-offs and practical constants

## Code Example
